'''Lakesis''' was a [[Dathomirian]] [[Gender|female]] [[Nightsisters|Nightsister]] [[witch]], one of three [[Great Mothers]] who allied with [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[Grand Admiral]] [[Mitth'raw'nuruodo]], and called [[Nightsisters|Nightsister]] [[Morgan Elsbeth]] and her [[Morgan Elsbeth's forces|forces]] to the [[planet]] [[Peridea]]. She, and the other Great Mothers predicted the arrival of [[Ahsoka Tano]], a [[Jedi Knight]] who would hinder their plans.<ref name="Part Six" /> After agreeing to use their [[Magick|dark magick]], Lakesis helped to locate Tano's ship and delayed her and her allies so their cargo could be transferred to the ''[[Chimaera]]''.<ref name="Part Seven">{{Ahsoka|Part Seven: Dreams and Madness}}</ref> She and her sisters then inducted Morgan into the sisterhood and [[Chant of Resurrection|resurrected]] the [[Night Trooper]]s killed by Tano, [[Sabine Wren]] and [[Ezra Bridger]] to hinder them. Lakesis was onboard the ''[[Eye of Sion]]'' when it went into [[hyperspace]] to travel back to the [[The galaxy|main galaxy]].<ref name="Part Eight">{{Ahsoka|Part Eight: The Jedi, the Witch, and the Warlord}}</ref>

{{Char-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
Lakesis first appeared in "[[Part Six: Far, Far Away]]," the sixth episode of the [[Star Wars: Ahsoka|''Ahsoka'' television series]], portrayed by [[Jane Edwina Seymour]]. While not identified in the episode, the character's name was given in the episode's credits.<ref name="Part Six" /> In [[wikipedia:Greek Mythology|Greek Mythology]], the similarly named [[wikipedia:Lachesis|Lachesis]] was one of three goddesses known as the [[wikipedia:Moirai|Fates]] who determined destiny itself, Lachesis specifically being the one of the three who determined how much of the thread of fate to allot.<ref name="Britannica">{{WebCite|url=https://www.britannica.com/topic/Fate-Greek-and-Roman-mythology|text=Fate|quote=Fate, [&hellip;] in Greek and Roman mythology, any of three goddesses who determined human destinies, and in particular the span of a person's life and his allotment of misery and suffering. [&hellip;] From the time of the poet Hesiod (8th century BC) on, however, the Fates were personified as three very old women who spin the threads of human destiny. Their names were Clotho (Spinner), Lachesis (Allotter), and Atropos (Inflexible). Clotho spun the "thread" of human fate, Lachesis dispensed it, and Atropos cut the thread (thus determining the individual's moment of death).|archivedate=20230609102218}}</ref> This is similar to how Lakesis and the other Great Mothers claimed to be able to read and know the threads of fate and destiny.<ref name="Part Six" />

